daemon: Windows-style palm handling, drop only the palm contact

Chris: make our palm handling like Windows. DisableOnPalm = true froze all
output and masked the click as soon as any contact failed the size / aspect
check, so a palm resting on the pad stopped a finger that was working next to
it, and a click with the palm down did nothing.

The Windows touchpad stack (strings TouchpadFingerUnderPalm, Palm_with_fingers,
TouchpadClickPlusPalmEventsNumber in the driver) keeps valid fingers and the
click working while a palm is on the pad. New [Touchpad] PalmMode (only used
with DisableOnPalm = true):

 - windows (default): the palm contacts (the ones failing SizeMin / SizeMax /
   AspectMin / AspectMax) are removed from the frame before anything else sees
   it. They never become a pointer, scroll or gesture finger and do not count
   towards the finger count (BTN_TOOL_*). The valid fingers keep their tracking
   ids and slots, so a palm landing or lifting causes no pointer jump, and the
   firmware click bit is processed as usual. The drag latch only looks at valid
   contacts, so a palm next to a held drag does not end it. Only when no valid
   finger is left is everything frozen and the click masked, as before.
 - freeze: the old behaviour, any palm freezes all output.

The size / aspect classification and the contact stability logic are
unchanged. A resting thumb at the bottom edge is not special cased: iptsd has
no thumb or edge zone concept, so that part is not implemented.

With DragDebug (or IPTSD_SL7_DRAG_DEBUG=1) every palm decision is logged:
palm-drop (palm contacts dropped, fingers and click stay active), palm-freeze
(no valid finger left, or PalmMode = freeze) and palm-end.

diff --git i/etc/iptsd.conf w/etc/iptsd.conf
index e31df2b..21fc8a6 100644
--- i/etc/iptsd.conf
+++ w/etc/iptsd.conf
@@ -40,6 +40,14 @@
 ##
 # DisableOnPalm = false

+##
+## What DisableOnPalm does when a palm (a contact failing the size / aspect checks) is on the
+## pad. windows: only the palm contacts are dropped, valid fingers and the physical click keep
+## working, and everything is frozen only while no valid finger is present (like the Windows
+## touchpad stack). freeze: any palm freezes all input and masks the click. (Default windows)
+##
+# PalmMode = windows
+
 ##
 ## How many centimeters a contact can be outside of the touchpad and still get registered.
 ##
diff --git i/src/apps/daemon/touch.hpp w/src/apps/daemon/touch.hpp
index 5573408..4442081 100644
--- i/src/apps/daemon/touch.hpp
+++ w/src/apps/daemon/touch.hpp
@@ -87,6 +87,13 @@ private:
 	// Whether all inputs will be lifted once a palm is registered.
 	bool m_disable_on_palm = false;

+	// Whether a palm only drops its own contact while a valid finger is present (PalmMode
+	// windows), instead of freezing all input (PalmMode freeze).
+	bool m_palm_windows = false;
+
+	// What the palm check decided in the last frame, for the debug log.
+	i32 m_palm_log_key = 0;
+
 	// The indices of the contacts in the current frame.
 	std::set<usize> m_current {};

@@ -280,6 +287,15 @@ public:

 			m_overshoot = config.touchpad_overshoot;
 			m_disable_on_palm = config.touchpad_disable_on_palm;
+
+			if (config.touchpad_palm_mode == "windows") {
+				m_palm_windows = true;
+			} else if (config.touchpad_palm_mode != "freeze") {
+				spdlog::warn("Unknown [Touchpad] PalmMode '{}', using 'windows'",
+				             config.touchpad_palm_mode);
+				m_palm_windows = true;
+			}
+
 			m_lift_grace_ms = config.touchpad_lift_grace_ms;
